Only let privileged callers create user namespaces

The stock GKI has USER_NS off, so no app can create user namespaces.
Turning it on for Droidspaces lets every unprivileged app create them,
which exposes kernel code that is otherwise unreachable without
privileges. Require CAP_SYS_ADMIN in the caller's user namespace, so
unprivileged apps are back to the stock behaviour while root
(Droidspaces) keeps working.

Side effect: non-root users inside a container cannot create user
namespaces either, so sandboxes that rely on unprivileged user
namespaces (Chromium, bubblewrap/Flatpak) do not work for them.

Ported from WildKernels/kernel_patches
common/droidspaces/0001-Guard-USER_NS-for-non-root-users.patch
(fatalcoder524, as of 55d0d2305d); only whitespace and a comment differ.

diff --git a/kernel/user_namespace.c b/kernel/user_namespace.c
index aa0b2e4..8f193d2 100644
--- a/kernel/user_namespace.c
+++ b/kernel/user_namespace.c
@@ -87,6 +87,10 @@ int create_user_ns(struct cred *new)
 	struct ucounts *ucounts;
 	int ret, i;
 
+	/* Only privileged callers may create user namespaces. */
+	if (!ns_capable(current_user_ns(), CAP_SYS_ADMIN))
+		return -EPERM;
+
 	ret = -ENOSPC;
 	if (parent_ns->level > 32)
 		goto fail;
@@ -178,6 +182,9 @@ int unshare_userns(unsigned long unshare_flags, struct cred **new_cred)
 	struct cred *cred;
 	int err = -ENOMEM;
 
+	if ((unshare_flags & CLONE_NEWUSER) && !ns_capable(current_user_ns(), CAP_SYS_ADMIN))
+		return -EPERM;
+
 	if (!(unshare_flags & CLONE_NEWUSER))
 		return 0;
 
